# John Dwyer & The Institute of Wow: The Complete Guide to His High-Impact Website Methodology

## TL;DR
- John "JD" Dwyer is an Australian direct-response marketing expert (founder of The Institute of Wow, Gold Coast) best known for convincing Jerry Seinfeld to front a campaign for the Greater Building Society; his core website doctrine is that a homepage must be a "24/7 sales machine," not a "brochure," built from an explicit 8-component homepage formula.
- The "8-point strategy" the user is looking for is real and documented on the Institute of Wow's "Website Makeovers" page: (1) problem/solution headline, (2) persuasive aggravating copy, (3) reinforcing images, (4) welcome video, (5) benefit copy/images, (6) call-to-action + data-capture, (7) video testimonials, (8) more reasons to buy. This sits inside his broader 5-step "Wheel of Wow" client-attraction formula, in which "Fix your website" is step 4.
- His method is built on incentive-based, direct-response marketing — "take their eyes off the price" with an "artificial wow factor" (the "adult Happy Meal toy"), use problem/solution messaging, capture data, and drive repeat business — and is sold via coaching (JD Private Consult), books (How To Wow, The Wow Manifesto, The Avalanche Leads Formula), done-for-you website makeovers, and incentive products (Fun Escapes vacation vouchers).

## Key Findings

**1. The 8-point website strategy exists and is explicit.** On the Institute of Wow "Website Makeovers" page, JD lists eight numbered "MUST-HAVE Components For Your Website Homepage." This is the closest thing to the "8 point website strategy" the user described. A separate, slightly shorter five-item list appears on the Institute's "Online Marketing" page.

**2. Website work is one pillar of a larger 5-step system** called the "Wheel of Wow" (sometimes the "5 Step Formula" or "client attraction system"): (1) identify your most profitable target audience; (2) create a wow factor to take eyes off price; (3) use problem/solution messaging; (4) fix your website (make it a 24/7 sales machine); (5) build repetitive trade (loyalty programs). His website philosophy can't be understood in isolation from this.

**3. His central marketing belief** is that competing on price is a losing game ("you are nuts if you set up a business based on price"), and that businesses must manufacture an "artificial wow factor" — an incentive unrelated to the product (like McDonald's Happy Meal toy) — to differentiate in a "sea of sameness."

**4. Credibility anchor: the Seinfeld campaign.** JD secured Jerry Seinfeld as spokesman for the Greater Building Society (Newcastle, NSW). The advertising campaign commenced on July 12, 2009 with a 60-second launch commercial; it was a three-year deal renewed annually, with Seinfeld's last spot in May 2012. The underlying offer (launched April 2001, well before Seinfeld) was "Get a home loan, get a free holiday." Per the Institute of Wow's own account, the Greater "DOUBLED their home loans in 90-days… and TRIPLED their home loans in 18 months" and were "the ONLY financial institution in the entire world to NEVER advertise an interest rate for 9 straight years."

## Details

### Background & Credentials
John Dwyer ("JD") was born and raised in Sydney, attended De La Salle Catholic schools, and earned advertising and marketing degrees. He worked in marketing for retailer Grace Bros at Roselands, then became National Marketing Manager at Woolworths in his twenties. He founded a Sydney advertising/marketing agency called Dynamic Ideas, which grew to about 25 staff and was contracted by News Ltd, the Nine Network, KFC, the NRL, and Westfield. His client list later included ACP Magazines, 7-Eleven, Coca-Cola, Video Ezy, Caltex, and McDonald's.

He moved into producing his own products with a trading-card business, gaining licenses from Disney, DreamWorks, Warner Bros, and Fox (themes such as Jurassic Park, Aladdin, Lion King, Batman Returns). By giving away millions of free cards through News Ltd Sunday newspapers, he grew NRL Rugby League card sales from $2 million to $12 million in a year. He later produced TV shows for the TEN Network including "Dreams Can Come True." He also recounts a major setback — losing $2.2 million in one week when a staffer printed trading cards in the wrong order, forcing a recall before Mother's Day and ultimately the loss of his home.

Today he runs The Institute of Wow from the Gold Coast, Queensland (ABN 89 832 380 633). He is married to Gael with six children. Per the Institute's "Wow Central" About page, "he has consulted to over 27,000 businesses and his Wow Marketing Formula has helped generate $15 Billion in extra sales for his clients" (the WOW Marketing Accelerator page adds "in more than 58 industries"). These are self-reported promotional figures and should be treated as marketing claims, not independently verified data.

### The Jerry Seinfeld / Greater Building Society Campaign
JD was marketing head/consultant for the Greater Building Society. He recommended the society front its campaign with a "high-profile global personality," drew up a shortlist, and put Seinfeld at the top because his "clean-cut yet cheeky" style matched the brand. The pitch was made through Seinfeld's manager George Shapiro; per the Newcastle Herald, "Mr Shapiro loved the Greater's proposal and emailed it to Seinfeld who responded a few days later to say he also loved the idea and was on board." It succeeded because, in JD's telling, "with guys of Jerry's stature, it's all about the creative" and they "simply asked Jerry to be Jerry." As the Newcastle Herald noted, "Seinfeld has only represented two other organisations in his career — Microsoft and American Express." Creative director was Paul Wilson of Loud (Sydney). The first commercial, "Maximum," featured Seinfeld doing observational stand-up outside a branch.

The underlying offer, predating Seinfeld, was the "Get a Home Loan & Get a Free Holiday" incentive — launched, per the Institute's case study, "In April 2001… in conjunction with the Leapfrog Group (a travel incentive company)." The Greater later added a 10-cents-per-litre petrol-savings bonus (a 52-voucher chequebook). The campaign generated enormous earned media (Greater's YouTube channel was briefly among Australia's most-watched, and US network CBS sent a crew to the New York shoot).

### Core Marketing Philosophy
- **"Take their eyes off the price."** The central metaphor. Use an incentive so the prospect stops comparing price. McDonald's Happy Meal toy is the archetype: parents "never get within $1 of what a Happy Meal costs because McDonald's took our eyes off the price."
- **Organic vs. artificial wow factor.** If you don't have an inherent wow product (iPhone, Rubik's Cube), you must create an "artificial wow factor" — typically an incentive unrelated to your product (free vacation, fuel savings, movie passes). JD calls his vacation voucher "an adult Happy Meal toy."
- **Incentive unrelated to product wins.** JD cites the Australian Direct Marketing Association (ADMA): the most successful direct-response campaigns give away something unrelated to the product, not more of the same product.
- **Direct response vs. brand/institutional advertising.** Per the Institute's "About Us," "John quotes that '95% of business don't use any of these direct response tactics.'" He argues big brands can afford pure brand-building but small businesses cannot, and should make everything measurable ("Ring. Ping. Ka-ching."). He still advocates marrying direct response with brand (citing Woolworths and Kellogg's).
- **"In a sea of sameness, stand out like a beacon."** Become "the UN of your industry"; find a genuine USP. "Most people think they have a Unique Selling Point but they don't."
- **Problem/Solution formula.** Lead with the prospect's problem, aggravate it, then present your product as the solution (his examples: Nurofen/headache ads, Jenny Craig). On a website this becomes the headline.
- **Be the expert / authority positioning** ("the Steve Irwin or MasterChef of your industry").
- **Repetitive trade / loyalty.** One sale isn't enough; build reward schemes (the café "buy 9 coffees, get the 10th free" card) to create "raving fans." Collect a database — most offline businesses fail to.
- **Theming/"theatre."** Add theatre and ambience to a business (Disney, Johnny Rockets, themed restaurants); he applies the same "theming" to websites, turning "bland into grand."

### THE WEBSITE METHODOLOGY (the core of the user's request)

**JD's overarching website thesis:** Your website is now your "Director of First Impressions" — no longer the receptionist. He says "most business websites are woeful": no strong benefit headline, no welcome video, no testimonials, no offer/CTA — they "provide information but are not designed to sell." The home page is the most important page, equivalent to a magazine's front cover. The fix: turn an "information portal" into a "24/7 sales machine" (also called a "24/7 sales juggernaut" or "digital salesman"). He warns that the last people who should build your website are "graphic artists or geeks" — you want marketers, because "looking good" is not the same as "selling."

**The 8-Point Homepage Strategy (verbatim from the Institute of Wow "Website Makeovers" page — "Your MUST-HAVE Components For Your Website Homepage"):**

1. **A compelling problem/solution headline** that evokes intrigue and captures the prospect's attention.
2. **Persuasive explanatory copy under the headline** that sends "chilling thoughts" through your prospects' minds and aggravates the problem.
3. **Images to "nail down" the message** for maximum impact (if the headline and copy aren't enough).
4. **A welcome video** that quickly explains the benefits your products/services provide. (If you're uncomfortable on camera, use a representative or a "doodle"/fast-drawing whiteboard video.)
5. **Benefits told through images and copy** — spell out what the prospect gains.
6. **Call to Action and/or a data-capture facility** such as a free report — the goal is to capture contact details of as many visitors as possible. He notes they integrate with CRMs such as Infusionsoft, Aweber, and Mailchimp.
7. **Video testimonials** from clients saying how good your products/services are.
8. **More compelling reasons** why they should support your business.

**The alternate (5-item) homepage checklist** on the Institute's "Online Marketing" page is a compressed version of the same idea:
- a. A benefit-driven headline and supportive copy (the problem/solution tactic).
- b. A welcome video (gets straight to the point — their problem, your solution).
- c. A Free Report / free download that visitors must give their contact details to receive.
- d. The 3 or 5 biggest benefits of dealing with you.
- e. Video testimonials from ecstatic clients.

In interviews JD repeats the same homepage recipe: a benefit/problem-solution headline (his real-estate example: "Would you like to sell your home within 11 days? We just did that"), a welcome video, three-to-five biggest benefits, a free download with opt-in, and testimonial videos on the home page (not buried on a testimonials page, because "no one goes to the testimonial page"). His own site's headline: "If your sales are flat line and you'd like to stimulate them, then you're at the right place." He stresses not wasting the space "above the fold."

**Done-for-you website service.** JD designs the components and "look"; a partner "direct-response website company" / "secret weapon design studio" executes. The Institute's Online Marketing page states verbatim: "We can provide you with a 5 page website (fully coded) for AUD$2,997." Turnaround is marketed as "weeks." A related division, **"Wow Automation,"** provides automated email and text-message follow-up ("a 'set and forget' marketing juggernaut") with templates sent to a database on a schedule to re-engage prospects and dormant past customers (he cites the principle that selling to a former customer is ~7x cheaper than acquiring a new one). Note: the Wow Automation page also makes an unverified promotional claim of "400% to 500% instant increases of sales" from "some clients."

**Website makeover case examples** (before/after on the "Website Makeover Samples" page) include an underground opal-mine tourism attraction re-themed Indiana-Jones-style ("Lost Treasure"/Opal Mine Adventure) that JD says skyrocketed revenue by widening the audience from seniors to families; plus makeovers for brands including The Great Australian Squeeze (orange juice farm), John Formica (Disney consultant), Aqua Action Slides (pool slides), Care Calling, Hello Harry (burgers), Kozco (air-con/solar), Waldemar (jewellery), and Fresh Homes (builder). A gazebo/Bali-hut company site (Tropical Lifestyle) was re-themed as a "backyard water park." (Most client names are derived from image file names rather than visible body text.)

